13. Complexity — straightforward solution

        There can be exponentially many partitions.
                
                For a string of length n, there are:
                
                               2^{n-1}        
                
                possible ways to place or not place partition boundaries.
                
        However, not all of them are valid palindrome partitions.
                
                For each candidate substring we may perform an O(n) palindrome check.
                
         So a safe upper-bound analysis for the straightforward implementation is roughly:
        O(n^2 2^n)

        depending on how you account for substring creation and palindrome checking.

        The important interview point is:        
                        The search space is exponential, and repeatedly checking substrings adds another factor.
        
        Auxiliary recursion/path depth is: O(n)   excluding output.


