14. L6-level optimization: precompute palindrome information

                Now comes the important improvement.
                
                Observe what happens in the naive solution.
                
                We repeatedly ask:                
                        Is s[i...j] a palindrome?

        The same substring may be checked many times through different recursion paths.
        
        That suggests precomputation.
        
        We can build:
                        boolean[][] palindrome
        where :
                palindrome[i][j]
        means:        
                s.substring(i, j + 1) is a palindrome.
        
        Then palindrome validation becomes:
        
                if (!palindrome[start][end]) {
                    continue;
                }
        
        which is:
                O(1)
                
        instead of scanning the substring.
                
        This is a classic transition from:
                
                Backtracking
                
                to:                
                        Backtracking + DP preprocessing
                
        🔥 This is exactly the bridge we're looking for before moving deeper into DP.
========================================================================================================================================
15. How do we build the palindrome DP?

                The recurrence is:                
                        palindrome[i][j] = (s[i] == s[j]) \land (j-i \le 2 \text{ or } palindrome[i+1][j-1])
                
                Why?                
                        A string is a palindrome when:                
                                first character == last character
                
                and the inside is also a palindrome.

                For example:                
                                "ababa"
                
                requires:                
                                a == a
                
                and:                
                                "bab"
                
                must also be palindrome.
                
 Base cases:

                One character
                                "a"
                always palindrome.

                Two characters
                                "aa"
                is palindrome when both characters are equal.

        That's why:        
                        j - i <= 1        
        can also be used in a slightly different recurrence formulation.
