        Auxiliary space:
        
                        Palindrome DP = O(n²)
                        Recursion/path = O(n)
        
        so: O(n^2)
        
                excluding output.
        
                The result itself can be exponential.
========================================================================================================================================
19. Why this is a very important problem for your DSA roadmap

                This problem is not just "another backtracking problem."
                
                                It introduces the first major connection between backtracking and DP:

                Palindrome Partitioning
                         │
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             ↓                       ↓
       Backtracking                 DP
             │                       │
      "What partitions?"       "Is s[i..j] palindrome?"
             │                       │
             └───────────┬───────────┘
                         ↓
              Backtracking + DP

The backtracking part answers:
                                Which partitions should I explore?

The DP part answers:
                        Can this candidate substring be accepted in O(1)?

That is a much more sophisticated use of DP than simply replacing recursion with a table.
